      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $0$: Zero is included by the subscript $0$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\sqrt{2}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-3$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $0$; $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
